  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "What is dynamic programming and how does it differ from other problem-solving techniques?",
      "answer": "Dynamic programming is an algorithmic paradigm that solves complex problems by breaking them down into simpler subproblems. Unlike greedy algorithms, which make locally optimal choices, dynamic programming considers all possible solutions to find the globally optimal one. It's especially useful for optimization problems and can significantly improve efficiency in solving certain types of computational challenges.",
      "faq_order": "1"
    },
    {
      "question": "What types of problems will I learn to solve using dynamic programming?",
      "answer": "The course covers a range of dynamic programming problem types, including:\n<ol style=\"margin-left: 24px\">\n<li>Maximum sum subarray</li>\n<li>Longest increasing subsequence</li>\n<li>Cut ribbon optimization</li>\n<li>Triangle sum problems</li>\n<li>Magic rankings</li>\n<li>These problems help you understand various applications of dynamic programming in real-world scenarios.</li>\n</ol>",
      "faq_order": "2"
    },
    {
      "question": "Are there any prerequisites I should know before starting to learn dynamic programming?",
      "answer": "Before diving into dynamic programming, it's helpful to have a solid foundation in:\n<ol style=\"margin-left: 24px\">\n<li>Basic programming concepts and at least one programming language</li>\n<li>Data structures (arrays, linked lists, trees, graphs)</li>\n<li>Algorithms (sorting, searching)</li>\n<li>Recursion</li>\n<li>Time and space complexity analysis</li>\n<li>Basic math and logical thinking skills</li>\n</ol>",
      "faq_order": "3"
    },
    {
      "question": "What are the key components of a dynamic programming solution?",
      "answer": "A dynamic programming solution typically consists of three main components:\n<ol style=\"margin-left: 24px\">\n<li>Optimal substructure: The problem can be broken down into smaller, similar subproblems.</li>\n<li>Overlapping subproblems: The same subproblems are solved multiple times.</li>\n<li>Memoization or tabulation: Storing solutions to subproblems to avoid redundant calculations.</li>\n</ol>",
      "faq_order": "4"
    }
  ],
